WEBVTT

NOTE
Journey: Advocates — Week 25 Leader Video
Unit 7, Lesson 1: Evil

1
00:00:06.320 --> 00:00:12.320
I want you to take a second and just take a deep breath.

2
00:00:12.320 --> 00:00:17.320
We are going to be talking about evil and pain and suffering.

3
00:00:17.320 --> 00:00:25.449
This may seem like it's just another lesson in the full gamut of lessons and you may

4
00:00:25.449 --> 00:00:26.320
be tired.

5
00:00:26.320 --> 00:00:35.320
The year is coming to an end and this is going to take some tenacity to get through.

6
00:00:35.320 --> 00:00:42.320
I want you to pray and I want you to pray for the next four weeks for your students.

7
00:00:42.320 --> 00:00:47.474
Not that you haven't been already, but if it's been hit or miss,

8
00:00:47.474 --> 00:00:53.837
I want you to really dial in on prayer this coming week in these next couple of

9
00:00:53.837 --> 00:00:54.320
weeks.

10
00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:59.416
Not only that you have students probably that are coming along and maybe are on the

11
00:00:59.416 --> 00:01:01.320
verge of making some decisions.

12
00:01:01.320 --> 00:01:03.320
I pray that that is the case.

13
00:01:03.320 --> 00:01:09.422
But you also have just some students that are going to have some significant

14
00:01:09.422 --> 00:01:10.225
questions,

15
00:01:10.225 --> 00:01:14.320
doubts, pain, hurt brought up these next few weeks.

16
00:01:14.320 --> 00:01:18.345
I want you to be aware of that before we even get started that we're going to dive

17
00:01:18.345 --> 00:01:22.320
into some pretty deep and some pretty heavy things over the next couple of weeks.

18
00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:24.320
I want you to be ready for it.

19
00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:29.500
So as you prepare for this week's discussion on evil,

20
00:01:29.500 --> 00:01:37.320
I want you to be prayerful for yourself, for your students and for your leaders.

21
00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:39.837
So when we're talking about evil,

22
00:01:39.837 --> 00:01:45.939
we're actually talking about two different approaches to how we're talking about

23
00:01:45.939 --> 00:01:46.320
evil.

24
00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.138
And throughout the next couple of weeks,

25
00:01:48.138 --> 00:01:50.320
we're going to look at both of these approaches.

26
00:01:50.320 --> 00:01:54.320
So I want you to be patient because there's two different ways that we can do this.

27
00:01:54.320 --> 00:02:01.320
There is what we call, what I call the philosophical, why can't I spell?

28
00:02:01.320 --> 00:02:03.600
We'll just quote that.

29
00:02:03.600 --> 00:02:08.600
The second is the pastoral approach.

30
00:02:08.600 --> 00:02:08.853
Now,

31
00:02:08.853 --> 00:02:13.905
the philosophical approach is sort of academic and the pastoral approach is just

32
00:02:13.905 --> 00:02:14.600
what it is.

33
00:02:14.600 --> 00:02:15.600
It's pastoral.

34
00:02:15.600 --> 00:02:18.600
So we're going to look at it from both sides.

35
00:02:18.600 --> 00:02:22.287
So I want you to make sure that you understand that at the starting gate,

36
00:02:22.287 --> 00:02:23.600
so you don't jump the gun.

37
00:02:23.600 --> 00:02:26.637
Because it's important that we see it from both sides,

38
00:02:26.637 --> 00:02:30.237
but not every student is going to respond to it from both sides,

39
00:02:30.237 --> 00:02:32.600
which is why we do it this particular way.

40
00:02:32.600 --> 00:02:37.600
So this first lesson we want students to really understand what evil is.

41
00:02:37.600 --> 00:02:40.600
Evil is the opposite of good.

42
00:02:40.600 --> 00:02:42.600
It's the opposite of God.

43
00:02:42.600 --> 00:02:46.962
And a lot of people will sort of assert that evil is this sort of thing that maybe

44
00:02:46.962 --> 00:02:47.600
God created.

45
00:02:47.600 --> 00:02:52.298
That maybe God did something where part of the whole creative universe that there's

46
00:02:52.298 --> 00:02:53.600
this force called evil.

47
00:02:53.600 --> 00:02:57.600
And it's the opposite of good and good's a thing and evil's a thing.

48
00:02:57.600 --> 00:03:00.600
Evil is the absence of good.

49
00:03:00.600 --> 00:03:02.600
It's a privation of good.

50
00:03:02.600 --> 00:03:03.600
It's sort of the same thing.

51
00:03:03.600 --> 00:03:05.600
We have darkness, right?

52
00:03:05.600 --> 00:03:08.600
We don't have a measurement for darkness.

53
00:03:08.600 --> 00:03:09.600
The measurement is light.

54
00:03:09.600 --> 00:03:13.600
And darkness is what we call the absence of light.

55
00:03:13.600 --> 00:03:15.600
And the same thing works for cold and heat.

56
00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:18.600
We don't have a measurement for cold.

57
00:03:18.600 --> 00:03:21.600
We have a measurement for the absence of heat.

58
00:03:21.600 --> 00:03:23.600
And that's what we call cold.

59
00:03:23.600 --> 00:03:25.600
We call it the absence of heat.

60
00:03:25.600 --> 00:03:26.600
We call it cold.

61
00:03:26.600 --> 00:03:27.600
It's just how we describe it.

62
00:03:27.600 --> 00:03:29.600
Evil is the same way.

63
00:03:29.600 --> 00:03:31.600
You remove God from the picture.

64
00:03:31.600 --> 00:03:33.600
You remove his goodness from the picture.

65
00:03:33.600 --> 00:03:37.600
And you suddenly have what we would call evil.

66
00:03:37.600 --> 00:03:44.645
And so very philosophically and kind of heady like sort of idea is that evil is this

67
00:03:44.645 --> 00:03:50.600
sort of other than God-ness out there that humanity is responsible for.

68
00:03:50.600 --> 00:03:57.187
And we're responsible since the garden because we actually allowed evil to permeate

69
00:03:57.187 --> 00:04:00.600
our world because we made the wrong choice.

70
00:04:00.600 --> 00:04:04.600
We made a choice to remove God from the picture.

71
00:04:04.600 --> 00:04:09.600
So evil is a stark reminder that we've lost our way.

72
00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:13.600
That we've looked to ourselves for our own moral compass.

73
00:04:13.600 --> 00:04:18.600
That we've told God that we've got this figured out and we don't need him.

74
00:04:18.600 --> 00:04:22.600
So being the gracious God that he is, you have free will.

75
00:04:22.600 --> 00:04:24.600
You can do what you want.

76
00:04:24.600 --> 00:04:26.600
You can choose to go your own way.

77
00:04:26.600 --> 00:04:28.600
So we did.

78
00:04:28.600 --> 00:04:32.600
And evil was the result of that choice.

79
00:04:32.600 --> 00:04:37.763
So one way I think that your students can really grasp this idea is by the

80
00:04:37.763 --> 00:04:38.600
storyteller.

81
00:04:38.600 --> 00:04:42.600
So you want someone that can tell a great story about being lost.

82
00:04:42.600 --> 00:04:48.075
And for some of them might not be too hard to find someone that's been lost but

83
00:04:48.075 --> 00:04:49.600
didn't use navigation.

84
00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:50.161
We all have,

85
00:04:50.161 --> 00:04:53.993
I don't remember the last time I actually got lost because all I got to do is plug

86
00:04:53.993 --> 00:04:54.600
it into here.

87
00:04:54.600 --> 00:05:00.600
But what if you had to navigate by the sun or by a compass?

88
00:05:00.600 --> 00:05:09.600
So you have north and then you have south and you have west and you have east.

89
00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:13.600
How do I know if I need to head east?

90
00:05:13.600 --> 00:05:16.600
How do I know which way is east?

91
00:05:16.600 --> 00:05:23.600
Well, the only way in which I know where east is is if I know where north is.

92
00:05:23.600 --> 00:05:27.632
So the first thing you do when you're lost and you have a compass or you can look at

93
00:05:27.632 --> 00:05:29.600
the sun is you figure out where north is.

94
00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.707
And once you find what's called magnetic north,

95
00:05:32.707 --> 00:05:37.600
then you can orient yourself and you can figure out every other direction.

96
00:05:37.600 --> 00:05:45.600
Well, imagine if the compass just kept being pointed back to you.

97
00:05:45.600 --> 00:05:46.320
What would you do?

98
00:05:46.320 --> 00:05:49.600
You'd just be walking in circles trying to figure out where you're supposed to go.

99
00:05:49.600 --> 00:05:54.600
You'd never be able to find east or west or south for that matter.

100
00:05:54.600 --> 00:06:00.600
It has to be something external of you, something that's not you to do that.

101
00:06:00.600 --> 00:06:03.600
So God serves as that moral compass.

102
00:06:03.600 --> 00:06:05.600
So what that leads us to conclude,

103
00:06:05.600 --> 00:06:08.600
and this is where the philosophical piece comes in,

104
00:06:08.600 --> 00:06:16.600
is the very fact that evil exists suggests that God exists.

105
00:06:16.600 --> 00:06:18.600
Think of it this way.

106
00:06:18.600 --> 00:06:21.600
If there is no good, then there is no evil.

107
00:06:21.600 --> 00:06:22.600
If there's no God, there's no good.

108
00:06:22.600 --> 00:06:24.600
If there's no good, there's no evil.

109
00:06:24.600 --> 00:06:27.600
Because if there's no good, then there's no evil.

110
00:06:27.600 --> 00:06:29.600
That means there's nothing to compare it to.

111
00:06:29.600 --> 00:06:33.479
The only way in which we understand what evil is is because we have good because we

112
00:06:33.479 --> 00:06:34.600
can look at evil and go,

113
00:06:34.600 --> 00:06:36.600
that's not good.

114
00:06:36.600 --> 00:06:39.600
So if that doesn't exist, then God doesn't exist.

115
00:06:39.600 --> 00:06:41.600
But there is evil.

116
00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.600
Therefore, there's good.

117
00:06:42.600 --> 00:06:45.600
Therefore, there's the standard to which we compare good and evil to.

118
00:06:45.600 --> 00:06:48.600
Therefore, God exists is kind of how that works.

119
00:06:48.600 --> 00:06:50.600
Hopefully that made sense.

120
00:06:50.600 --> 00:06:54.623
So from a philosophical standpoint,

121
00:06:54.623 --> 00:07:00.600
the very existence of evil suggests that God exists.

122
00:07:00.600 --> 00:07:06.600
Because we have a standard to which we can understand what evil actually is.

123
00:07:06.600 --> 00:07:08.600
Okay, so small group time.

124
00:07:08.600 --> 00:07:10.600
You're going to get questions.

125
00:07:10.600 --> 00:07:13.600
It's going to get real, real fast.

126
00:07:13.600 --> 00:07:17.620
So this is the point where students are going to start bringing up probably a load

127
00:07:17.620 --> 00:07:18.600
of questions on you,

128
00:07:18.600 --> 00:07:20.600
like, well, why does God allow suffering?

129
00:07:20.600 --> 00:07:23.600
Why does God allow good people to suffer?

130
00:07:23.600 --> 00:07:25.600
Why do bad things happen to good people?

131
00:07:25.600 --> 00:07:30.875
And any other variation of the question that basically says,

132
00:07:30.875 --> 00:07:33.600
why is there evil in suffering?

133
00:07:33.600 --> 00:07:36.600
Some of it you can probably answer as it relates to evil specifically.

134
00:07:36.600 --> 00:07:38.600
Some of it you may want to table.

135
00:07:38.600 --> 00:07:40.600
But be careful how you table a question.

136
00:07:40.600 --> 00:07:41.600
Make sure you write it down.

137
00:07:41.600 --> 00:07:44.600
Write it in your leader's guide, your discussion guide, or wherever you have to.

138
00:07:44.600 --> 00:07:47.600
So you can come back to it so you don't ignore the question.

139
00:07:47.600 --> 00:07:49.600
But there's going to be a lot of questions.

140
00:07:49.600 --> 00:07:52.279
And if you're not getting a lot of questions,

141
00:07:52.279 --> 00:07:54.600
your students still have the questions.

142
00:07:54.600 --> 00:07:56.600
So I would suggest posing them yourself.

143
00:07:56.600 --> 00:08:00.600
Ask them the questions that they're probably asking you.

144
00:08:00.600 --> 00:08:03.600
So if no one says, why has God allowed suffering?

145
00:08:03.600 --> 00:08:05.600
Or why has God allowed good people to suffer?

146
00:08:05.600 --> 00:08:07.600
Why has God allowed a child to die?

147
00:08:07.600 --> 00:08:10.600
Why has God allowed a lot of things that we kind of look at and go, oh my gosh,

148
00:08:10.600 --> 00:08:13.600
what an injustice, what a terrible thing?

149
00:08:13.600 --> 00:08:16.600
You ask it and see what they think.

150
00:08:16.600 --> 00:08:21.600
But be ready because this is going to start getting very, very personal

151
00:08:21.600 --> 00:08:23.600
and very deep for some students,

152
00:08:23.600 --> 00:08:27.600
especially if they have experienced any kind of evil or suffering in their lives.

